# A written argument keeps its name. `f(a: 2)` names the parameter the pipe has
# already filled, so this is refused, and *that it is refused at all* is the
# assertion, deliberately not which diagnostic says so.
#
# Splitting it that way is what gives `rebuild-arguments-positionally` an
# assertion of its own. Rebuilding the list positionally drops the name and the
# call quietly compiles; appending the left side instead leaves the name in
# place and the program is still refused, for a different reason. Pinning the
# message here would let both mutants redden one assertion, and an assertion
# two mutants can redden is owned by neither.
fn f(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int = a * 10 + b

pub fn dup(n: Int) -> Int = n |> f(a: 2)
